## Solution

**Setup and recurrence.** Number the positions in the current list $1,2,\dots,m$. One "round" erases every position $\equiv 1\pmod 3$, i.e. positions $1,4,7,\dots$. The survivors keep their order. Let $g(m)$ denote the position (in the current list of size $m$) of the number that is erased *last* over the whole process. Since the original list is $1,2,\dots,2002$, position equals value, so the answer is $g(2002)$.

**Size reduction.** A round on a list of size $m$ removes $\lceil m/3\rceil$ elements and leaves
$$m'=\left\lfloor \tfrac{2m}{3}\right\rfloor$$
elements. (Check the three residues: $m=3r,3r+1,3r+2$ give $m'=2r,2r,2r+1=\lfloor 2m/3\rfloor$.)

**Position map.** The surviving positions, in order, are $2,3,5,6,8,9,\dots$ — i.e. for each block $\{3k-2,3k-1,3k\}$ the last two survive. So the element at new position $q$ came from old position
$$h(q)=\begin{cases}3k-1,& q=2k-1\ (\text{odd}),\\[2pt] 3k,& q=2k\ (\text{even}),\end{cases}\qquad\text{equivalently } h(q)=3\lceil q/2\rceil-\mathbf 1_{q\text{ odd}}.$$
This holds for every $q\in\{1,\dots,m'\}$ regardless of $m\bmod 3$ (verified for $m=3r,3r+1,3r+2$; in the $3r+2$ case the extra survivor $3r+2$ is exactly $h(2r+1)$).

**Recurrence.** The element that is last-erased in a list of size $m$ is the image, under $h$, of the element last-erased in the reduced list of size $m'$:
$$g(m)=h\!\big(g(\lfloor 2m/3\rfloor)\big),\qquad g(1)=1,\; g(2)=2,\; g(3)=3.$$
(Quick checks: $g(4)=h(g(2))=h(2)=3$; $g(5)=h(g(3))=h(3)=5$; $g(8)=h(g(5))=h(5)=8$; $g(9)=h(g(6))=h(5)=8$. A direct simulation of $n=14$ gives last-erased $12$, matching $g(14)=h(g(9))=h(8)=12$.)

**Iterating down from $2002$.** The chain of sizes is
$$2002\to1334\to889\to592\to394\to262\to174\to116\to77\to51\to34\to22\to14\to9\to6\to4\to2.$$
(Each entry is $\lfloor 2\cdot(\text{previous})/3\rfloor$; e.g. $\lfloor 4004/3\rfloor=1334$, $\lfloor 2668/3\rfloor=889$, …, $\lfloor 8/3\rfloor=2$.)

**Working back up** from $g(2)=2$, repeatedly applying $h$:

| $m$ | $g(m')$ | $q$ parity | $g(m)=h(q)$ |
|---|---|---|---|
| 4 | 2 | even | 3 |
| 6 | 3 | odd | 5 |
| 9 | 5 | odd | 8 |
| 14 | 8 | even | 12 |
| 22 | 12 | even | 18 |
| 34 | 18 | even | 27 |
| 51 | 27 | odd | 41 |
| 77 | 41 | odd | 62 |
| 116 | 62 | even | 93 |
| 174 | 93 | odd | 140 |
| 262 | 140 | even | 210 |
| 394 | 210 | even | 315 |
| 592 | 315 | odd | 473 |
| 889 | 473 | odd | 710 |
| 1334 | 710 | even | 1065 |
| 2002 | 1065 | odd | $3\cdot533-1=1598$ |

(For odd $q=2k-1$: $h=3k-1$; for even $q=2k$: $h=3k$. E.g. $q=1065=2\cdot533-1\Rightarrow h=3\cdot533-1=1598$.)

Every $g(m)\le m$, so each application stays within the valid range of $h$.

**Answer.** The last number to be erased is $\boxed{1598}$.

